"""Filler turns for the generated scenarios: realistic side questions and small
asides that do not touch any planted fact. Each entry is a (user, assistant) pair.
``<P>`` is replaced by the project name. No pool entry may mention a decision,
a rejected option, a planted value or the abandoned folder (a test checks this).
"""
from __future__ import annotations

CODING_FILLERS = [
    ("Quick tangent: should I be logging the full request body when something fails, or is that a bad idea?",
     "Log the identifiers and the failure reason, not the whole body. Bodies can hold customer phone numbers and addresses, and they bloat the logs. If you need the body for debugging, log it at DEBUG behind a flag you can switch on temporarily."),
    ("What status code should the endpoint return if the JSON is malformed? I've seen 400 and 422 used.",
     "400 is the safe choice for 'I could not parse this at all'. 422 fits better when the JSON parses but fails validation. Carriers rarely care which one you pick, as long as it is a 4xx so they do not treat it as a server fault."),
    ("Can you remind me why we call `request.get_json(silent=True)` in some places and not others?",
     "`silent=True` returns None instead of raising when the body is not valid JSON, so you can return your own error message. Without it Flask raises a 400 for you. Either is fine, but pick one and use it everywhere in <P> so the behaviour is predictable."),
    ("Is it worth adding type hints to the notification helpers? The team is split on it.",
     "For small, stable helpers it is cheap and pays off when you refactor. Start with function signatures only, skip the clever generics, and let mypy run in CI in non-blocking mode for a week to see how noisy it is."),
    ("My ruff run is complaining about an unused import in a file I didn't touch. Ignore it?",
     "Fix it in a separate commit so the review of the real change stays clean. `ruff check --fix` will remove it safely. If the same warning keeps coming back, check whether a star-import is re-exporting the name."),
    ("What's a sensible request timeout when we call the SMS gateway from the service?",
     "Set a connect timeout and a read timeout separately, a couple of seconds for connect and somewhat longer for read. A single large number hides problems. Also make sure the timeout surfaces as a logged warning, not a silent drop."),
    ("Do I need to worry about timezone handling for the `received_at` timestamps?",
     "Store everything in UTC and convert only when you render something for a person. Use timezone-aware datetimes in Python so you never compare naive and aware values, which fails at runtime and is annoying to track down."),
    ("The logging output is in three different formats across the app. Is there a quick way to unify it?",
     "Configure logging once at app start with a single formatter, and have modules call `logging.getLogger(__name__)` instead of `print` or their own handlers. A JSON formatter is nice if your log platform parses it, otherwise a plain key=value line is enough."),
    ("Thanks, that helps. Unrelated: how should I name the test functions so failures read well in CI?",
     "Name them after the behaviour, for example `test_unknown_status_is_ignored`, not after the function under test. When a test fails in CI the name is often the only thing people read, so it should say what broke."),
    ("Can you explain what a Flask blueprint actually buys us in a service this small?",
     "A blueprint groups routes, error handlers and templates so you can register them under a prefix and test them in isolation. For one endpoint it is optional, but it keeps the app factory tidy and makes it easy to add a health check route later."),
    ("Should the health check endpoint touch the database?",
     "Keep two probes if you can: a cheap liveness check that only returns 200, and a readiness check that runs `SELECT 1`. If the orchestrator restarts pods on a failing liveness probe, a slow database should not cause restart loops."),
    ("I keep forgetting how to run a single test with a debugger attached. Remind me?",
     "Run it with `pytest -x --pdb path::test_name` to drop into the debugger on the first failure, or put `breakpoint()` in the code and run with `-s` so pytest does not swallow the prompt."),
    ("What's the cleanest way to freeze time in tests for anything that uses `datetime.now`?",
     "Inject the clock: pass a `now` callable into the function and default it to `datetime.now`. That avoids patching globals. If injecting is too invasive, `freezegun` works, but it is a heavier hammer and slows large suites slightly."),
    ("Is it okay to catch a bare `Exception` around the notification call so one bad customer doesn't break the batch?",
     "Catch it at the boundary where you can do something useful, log it with the traceback and the customer id, and continue. Do not swallow it silently, and let `KeyboardInterrupt` and `SystemExit` through, which a bare `except Exception` already does."),
    ("Can you suggest a sane layout for the tests folder? Mine has grown organically.",
     "Mirror the package: `tests/test_<module>.py` for unit tests, a `tests/conftest.py` for shared fixtures, and a `tests/fixtures/` folder for static payloads. Keep slow or network tests behind a marker so the default run stays fast."),
    ("We got a question from the security team about dependency pinning. What do you recommend?",
     "Pin direct dependencies in a requirements file with exact versions, generate a lock file with hashes if your tooling supports it, and let a bot open update PRs weekly. That gives reproducible builds without letting versions rot."),
    ("What's the difference between `raise` and `raise e` inside an except block? A reviewer flagged it.",
     "A bare `raise` re-raises the active exception and keeps the original traceback intact. `raise e` also works in Python 3 but adds a frame to the traceback, which is noise. Prefer the bare form, or `raise NewError(...) from e` when you want to wrap."),
    ("Our CI takes a while to install dependencies. Any cheap wins?",
     "Cache the pip download directory keyed on the hash of the requirements file, and install with `--no-deps` once the lock file is trusted. Splitting dev-only tools into a separate file also helps jobs that only need the runtime."),
    ("Do you think I should add a CHANGELOG entry for this fix?",
     "If the project already keeps one, yes: one line saying what users of the service will notice, which here is fewer repeated customer messages. If there is no changelog, the PR description is enough, do not start a new convention for one fix."),
    ("Someone on the team suggested using dataclasses for the payload instead of dicts. Thoughts?",
     "A small frozen dataclass for the fields you actually use makes typos fail loudly and gives you autocomplete. Keep parsing in one function that builds it, and leave unknown fields in an `extra` dict so new carrier fields do not break you."),
    ("What does `ruff format` do differently from black? Is the diff going to be large?",
     "They are intentionally almost identical. On a codebase already formatted with black you will typically see a handful of whitespace changes around comments and parentheses. Run it once as its own commit so blame stays readable."),
    ("How verbose should the commit messages be for a change like this?",
     "A short imperative subject, then a paragraph of why: the symptom, the cause, and the approach. Reviewers can see what changed from the diff, so spend your words on the reasoning and any alternatives you ruled out."),
    ("I'm getting `RuntimeError: Working outside of application context` in a test. What am I missing?",
     "Something is touching `current_app` or `g` outside a request or app context. In tests, either use the `app.app_context()` block, or use the test client, which pushes a request context for you. A fixture that yields inside `with app.app_context():` is the usual fix."),
    ("Do you have an opinion on f-strings versus `logging` lazy formatting?",
     "For log calls, use the lazy form, `logger.info('sent %s', tracking_id)`. The string is only built if the level is enabled, and log platforms can group by the constant message. Everywhere else, f-strings are great."),
    ("The customer success team wants a daily count of notifications sent. Is that a separate project?",
     "It can be a small one. The cheapest start is a structured log line per notification plus a saved query in your log tool. If they later want a dashboard with history, you can add a counter metric, but do not build it before they confirm they need it."),
    ("Is `requests` fine for the outbound SMS call or should I move to `httpx`?",
     "`requests` is fine here. Move to `httpx` when you need async or HTTP/2, not before. Whichever you use, reuse a single `Session` so connections are pooled rather than opened for every message."),
    ("What's the polite way to tell a teammate their PR needs tests?",
     "Be specific and offer a path: point to the behaviour that is not covered, suggest the smallest test that would catch a regression, and offer to pair if the setup is awkward. Reviews land better when the ask is concrete."),
    ("How do I keep secrets out of the test output when a test fails and prints the environment?",
     "Never print the whole environment. Configure the settings object so its `repr` masks secret fields, and keep test credentials in a dedicated fixture with obviously fake values so a leaked log line is harmless."),
    ("Can I rely on dict ordering when building the JSON response?",
     "In Python 3.7+ dicts keep insertion order and Flask preserves it unless `JSON_SORT_KEYS` is set, but clients should never rely on key order in JSON. Treat it as cosmetic."),
    ("Anything I should know about running the app with gunicorn in production versus the dev server?",
     "Use gunicorn with a small number of sync or gthread workers, set a worker timeout a bit above your slowest legitimate request, and put it behind your ingress. The Flask dev server is single-purpose and not hardened."),
    ("Do you think a README section on local setup is worth the time?",
     "Yes, five lines: create the virtualenv, install requirements, set the environment variable names the app reads, run the tests, start the app. New contributors will thank you, and so will future you after a laptop swap."),
    ("What should the service do when the carrier sends a status we don't recognise?",
     "Log it at warning level with the raw status string, return 200 so the carrier does not keep retrying, and skip the customer notification. Then add the new status to the mapping when someone confirms what it means."),
    ("Is there a good rule of thumb for how long a function should be?",
     "Not a line count, but if you need a comment to explain a block inside a function, that block probably wants a name of its own. Functions that fit on one screen and do one thing are easier to test."),
    ("Can you explain what `pytest.mark.parametrize` is giving us over a for loop?",
     "Each parameter set becomes its own test case with its own pass or fail, so one bad input does not hide the others, and the failing case is named in the output. Use `ids=` to make the names readable."),
    ("I always forget the difference between a mock and a stub. Quick refresher?",
     "A stub just returns canned answers so the code under test can proceed. A mock also records how it was called so you can assert on that. Prefer stubs and assert on outcomes, and keep mocks for interactions that are themselves the behaviour."),
    ("Should the SMS template text live in code or in a config file?",
     "Keep it in a template file or constants module so copy changes do not need to touch logic, but still versioned in the repo so changes get reviewed. Only move it to a database when non-engineers need to edit it live."),
    ("We might add email notifications later. Does that change how I should structure the notify function?",
     "A little. Make the notifier take a channel argument, or define a small interface with a `send(customer, message)` method, and keep the status-to-message mapping separate from delivery. You do not need a plugin system, two implementations are enough to start."),
    ("Why does my editor show a different Python version than the terminal?",
     "The editor is probably pointed at a different interpreter than the activated virtualenv. Select the interpreter from the virtualenv's `bin` folder in the editor settings, then restart the language server so it re-reads the installed packages."),
    ("Can you give me a one-line explanation of idempotency I can put in the PR description?",
     "An operation is idempotent if doing it twice leaves the system in the same state as doing it once, so a retried request cannot cause a second side effect."),
    ("How noisy should a service be at startup? Mine prints about twenty lines.",
     "A few lines are useful: version, environment name, and which optional features are on. Anything beyond that belongs at DEBUG. Never print configuration values that could be secrets."),
    ("Is it normal for the first request after deploy to be slow?",
     "Yes, usually imports, connection pool warm-up and lazy initialisation. If it is long enough to trip upstream timeouts, add a warm-up step or readiness delay so traffic only arrives after the first successful self-check."),
    ("What would you check first if the service suddenly starts using more memory?",
     "Look for unbounded collections that grow with traffic, such as module-level lists or caches without a size limit, and for request bodies held in memory longer than needed. A heap snapshot from a staging run beats guessing."),
    ("Could you explain how the `before_request` hook differs from a decorator on the view?",
     "A `before_request` hook runs for every request to the app or blueprint it is registered on, so it suits cross-cutting work like request ids. A decorator applies only to the views you wrap, which is clearer for something like auth that some routes skip."),
    ("The product owner asked how confident we are that this fixes the complaints. What do I say?",
     "Say what you can show: with the change, replaying the same delivery twice produces one message, covered by an automated test. What you cannot show yet is real carrier behaviour in production, so suggest watching the support queue for a week."),
    ("Can I use `assert` for validating incoming data?",
     "No. Assertions are stripped when Python runs with `-O` and they raise `AssertionError`, which is not a useful response. Validate explicitly and return a proper 4xx."),
    ("Is there an easy way to see which of my routes are slowest locally?",
     "Wrap requests with a tiny timing middleware that logs the path and duration, or run the app under a profiler such as `pyinstrument` for a single request. The first approach is enough to spot an outlier."),
    ("A colleague asked if we need an OpenAPI spec for a single webhook. Do we?",
     "Not strictly. A short markdown description of the payload fields you rely on, with one example, gives most of the value. Write the spec when a second consumer or a client generator needs it."),
    ("What's your take on retry decorators around the outbound SMS call?",
     "Only retry errors that are likely transient, like timeouts and 5xx, with a small cap and some jitter. Do not retry 4xx. And remember retries multiply load during an outage, so keep the cap low."),
    ("Does it matter whether I return `jsonify(...)` or a plain tuple from the view?",
     "Both work. Returning a dict directly is the shortest in current Flask, it is converted to JSON for you. Use `jsonify` or `make_response` when you also need to set headers or an unusual status."),
    ("I want to leave a TODO in the code for later. Any convention?",
     "Include your name or a ticket reference and say what triggers the work, for example `# TODO(chirag): drop once the carrier v2 payloads are live`. A bare TODO tends to live forever."),
    ("Before I forget: what makes a good PR size for this team?",
     "Small enough to review in one sitting, roughly a few hundred changed lines at most, and with one purpose. If a refactor is needed to make the fix easy, send it first as its own PR."),
]

DATA_FILLERS = [
    ("Side question: when I plot tenure, should I use a histogram or a density plot?",
     "Start with a histogram with a sensible bin width, because it shows the actual counts and any odd spikes, such as an artefact at renewal dates. A density plot is nicer for comparing groups on one axis, but it can hide small sample sizes."),
    ("What's the quickest way to check for duplicates across the three tables after I join them?",
     "Count rows before and after each join and compare with the number of distinct keys. If a join that should be one-to-one grows the row count, the right-hand table has duplicate keys. `df.duplicated(subset=[...]).sum()` gives a quick number."),
    ("I'm seeing a lot of missing values in the plan column. Should I drop those rows?",
     "First find out why they are missing. If they all come from one acquisition channel or one date range, that is a data pipeline issue worth reporting, and dropping them would bias the sample. Fill with an explicit 'unknown' category if the rest of the row is useful."),
    ("Can you remind me how `pd.merge` behaves with `how='left'` when the right side has duplicate keys?",
     "Every matching right-hand row produces an output row, so a left row with three matches appears three times. That is the usual cause of unexpectedly inflated counts. Pass `validate='one_to_one'` or `'many_to_one'` so pandas raises if the assumption breaks."),
    ("Should I standardise the numeric features before fitting? A teammate said it's mandatory.",
     "It is not mandatory for every model, but it matters when you want to compare coefficient sizes or when you use regularisation, because the penalty depends on scale. Fit the scaler on the training split only and apply it to the rest."),
    ("What's an easy way to make my charts readable for non-technical readers?",
     "One message per chart, a title that states the finding rather than the variable names, direct labels instead of a legend where possible, and a restrained palette. Remove gridlines and borders that do not help you read values."),
    ("I'm not sure whether to report a mean or a median for revenue per customer.",
     "Revenue is usually right-skewed, so the median describes a typical customer better and the mean describes total revenue per customer. Report both and say which question each answers, or the finance team may mix them up."),
    ("How do I check if two groups differ meaningfully without over-engineering it?",
     "Report the difference with a confidence interval, using a bootstrap if you do not want to assume a distribution. A p-value alone says little about size, and the interval tells readers whether the difference could matter commercially."),
    ("My notebook keeps getting slower. Any habits that help?",
     "Restart and run all regularly so hidden state does not accumulate, cache expensive reads to a parquet file, and keep large intermediate frames out of the notebook namespace once you have saved them. Downcasting integer and category columns also saves memory."),
    ("Is it okay to use a random seed everywhere, or does that make the analysis less honest?",
     "Fixing the seed makes results reproducible, which is the honest option. Just do not tune the seed until the numbers look good. If a conclusion changes with the seed, report that instability instead of hiding it."),
    ("What is a good way to document the data cleaning steps for finance?",
     "A short table: step, rows before, rows after, reason. It makes the cleaning auditable and lets someone challenge a rule without reading code. Generate it from the notebook so it never drifts from what the code did."),
    ("Does it matter if I use `df.copy()` before modifying a slice?",
     "Yes, it avoids the `SettingWithCopyWarning` and, more importantly, the situation where you accidentally change the original frame or silently change nothing. Copy once at the point where you start a modifying section."),
    ("Can you explain the difference between `groupby().transform` and `groupby().agg`?",
     "`agg` collapses each group to one row. `transform` returns a result the same length as the input, aligned to the original index, which is useful for adding a per-group mean or rank as a new column without a merge."),
    ("I've got dates in three formats in one column. What now?",
     "Parse with an explicit format per pattern, using `pd.to_datetime(..., format=..., errors='coerce')`, then look at the rows that came out as NaT to see what you missed. Avoid relying on automatic inference because day-month ordering is ambiguous."),
    ("How much should I trust an AUC computed on one train/test split?",
     "A single split can be lucky or unlucky. Use cross-validation, or at least repeat the split a few times, and report the spread. If the time order matters, split by time so you are not testing on the past."),
    ("What's the simplest sanity check for a classification model that looks too good?",
     "Look for leakage: features that are only known after the outcome, identifiers that act as a proxy, or duplicated rows across train and test. Inspect the top features, if one dominates and is suspicious, remove it and see what happens."),
    ("Should I be worried about class imbalance here?",
     "It depends on the metric. Accuracy is misleading when one class is rare, so look at precision and recall for the rare class, and the precision-recall curve. You can reweight classes before resorting to resampling."),
    ("Quick one: how do I show uncertainty on a bar chart?",
     "Add error bars from a bootstrap or standard error, and say in the caption what they represent. For many categories, a dot plot with intervals is cleaner than bars."),
    ("The finance lead keeps asking for a single number. How do I push back kindly?",
     "Give the number first, then one line on what could move it and by roughly how much. Most people accept a range once they see it is attached to a decision, such as what budget to hold back."),
    ("Is it fine to save intermediate data as CSV?",
     "For sharing, yes. For your own pipeline, parquet keeps dtypes, is smaller and loads faster. If you must use CSV, write dates in ISO format and note the encoding."),
    ("Can you explain what a confusion matrix is telling me beyond accuracy?",
     "It splits errors into false positives and false negatives, which usually have different costs. In a retention setting, missing a customer who leaves is typically a different kind of mistake from flagging one who stays."),
    ("What chart would you use to compare cohorts over time?",
     "A line chart of the metric by months since start, one line per cohort, usually works best, with the most recent cohorts highlighted. A heatmap is a good alternative when there are many cohorts."),
    ("How can I make sure I'm not double counting customers who changed plans?",
     "Choose the grain explicitly: one row per customer, or one row per customer-plan period. Then count distinct customer ids for customer-level numbers. Changing grain halfway through a notebook is a common source of inflated totals."),
    ("Is it normal for my feature table to have more columns than I thought?",
     "It happens when one-hot encoding or date parts are added automatically. List the columns before fitting and check for near-constant ones, which add noise and can make coefficients unstable."),
    ("I want to annotate a line chart with an event, like a price change. Any tips?",
     "Add a thin vertical line at the date and a short label placed at the top, and make sure the label is in the same font size as the axis labels. One or two annotations are readable, ten become noise."),
    ("What's the best way to explain a p-value to someone in the business team?",
     "Say: if there were truly no difference, how often would we see a gap at least this big just by chance. Then give the effect size, because 'significant' alone does not tell them whether it matters."),
    ("Do I need to log-transform skewed features for a linear model?",
     "Often it helps, since coefficients are then about proportional changes, and extreme values stop dominating the fit. Check the histogram before and after, and handle zeros with `log1p` rather than dropping them."),
    ("A colleague asked me to share the notebook. What should I clean up first?",
     "Restart and run all to make sure it works from top to bottom, remove dead cells, put paths and parameters in one cell at the top, and clear outputs that contain customer-level data."),
    ("What should I do about outliers in the payment amounts?",
     "Investigate before removing: they may be legitimate annual plans or data errors such as a missing decimal. Winsorising for a model is fine if documented, but keep the raw values in the audit table."),
    ("Is there a quick way to profile a new dataset?",
     "`df.describe(include='all')`, null counts per column, the number of unique values per column and a look at the min and max of each date. That takes five minutes and catches most surprises."),
    ("How do I choose the number of bins for tenure buckets in a table?",
     "Pick buckets that match how the business talks about customers, such as first month, first quarter, first year, and check each bucket has enough customers. Data-driven bins are less communicable."),
    ("Can you explain survival curves in two sentences for the deck?",
     "A survival curve shows, for each point in time since signup, the share of customers still active. A steep early drop means people leave soon after starting, a gentle slope later means those who stay tend to stay."),
    ("Does the order of columns matter when I pass a DataFrame to scikit-learn?",
     "Models fit on a DataFrame remember the column names in recent versions and warn if they differ at prediction time, but older versions rely on order alone. Select columns explicitly with a saved list so training and scoring always match."),
    ("What font and size should I use for chart text in slides?",
     "Use the deck's body font, at least the size of the body text, so a chart is legible when projected. Avoid rotated labels if you can flip the chart to horizontal bars."),
    ("I got a warning about a convergence failure when fitting. Should I panic?",
     "No, but do not ignore it. Scale the features, increase the iteration limit, or loosen the tolerance and see whether the coefficients move. If they keep changing, the problem may be near-collinear features."),
    ("How do I check for multicollinearity quickly?",
     "Compute the correlation matrix and look for pairs above roughly 0.8, or compute variance inflation factors. Drop or combine one of each strongly related pair if you need stable coefficients."),
    ("Can I use `apply` with a lambda over rows, or is that too slow?",
     "It works but runs a Python loop. Prefer vectorised column operations, `np.where`, or `map` with a dictionary. On a few hundred thousand rows the difference is seconds versus minutes."),
    ("Is there a convention for naming derived columns?",
     "Use a clear unit or meaning in the name, such as `tenure_days` or `is_trial`, and keep booleans prefixed with `is_` or `has_`. Avoid abbreviations that only you will recognise in six months."),
    ("The deck needs a methodology slide. What should be on it?",
     "Data source and period, the main exclusions, how the key terms are defined, the method in one sentence, and the main limitation. Keep the detail in an appendix so the main flow is not slowed down."),
    ("Does it make sense to show the full distribution in the main deck or only the median?",
     "Show the median in the headline, and put the distribution next to it if the spread changes the decision. Otherwise move it to the appendix and mention that it is available."),
    ("How should I handle customers who joined very recently?",
     "They have had little time to leave, so including them can understate the rate. Either restrict the analysis to customers with a minimum observation window, or use a method designed for censored data."),
    ("What would you check if two supposedly identical totals differ by a small amount?",
     "Look for different filters, time zone boundaries at month ends, rounding done before versus after summing, and null handling. Reconcile by listing the rows present in one total but not the other."),
    ("Can you remind me how to set a seed in numpy and in scikit-learn?",
     "In numpy use `np.random.default_rng(seed)` and pass the generator around. In scikit-learn, pass `random_state=seed` to the estimator or splitter. Setting both makes the run repeatable."),
    ("Is a log scale fine for a chart going to executives?",
     "Only if you label it clearly and the audience is comfortable reading it. If not, show the percentage change instead, or break the axis into a table next to the chart."),
    ("My boss asked for a quick dashboard. Should I stop the analysis to build it?",
     "Ask what decision it should support first. A static chart in the deck often answers it. If they need a refreshing view, schedule it after the analysis is signed off so the definitions are final."),
    ("What's a reasonable way to impute missing numeric values?",
     "Start with the median and add an `is_missing` indicator column, which keeps the signal that something was absent. More elaborate imputation is rarely worth it unless a column is mostly populated and important."),
    ("How many significant figures should I show in the deck?",
     "As many as the precision justifies, typically one decimal for percentages. Showing four decimals suggests accuracy the data does not have, though keep full precision in the appendix table."),
    ("Is it better to use seaborn or matplotlib directly?",
     "Seaborn is quicker for statistical plots with sensible defaults, matplotlib gives you full control for the final polish. Many people draw with seaborn and tweak the axes with matplotlib."),
    ("Someone asked whether we should look at seasonality. Where do I begin?",
     "Plot the metric by calendar month across years if you have them, or month-over-month changes, and see if the pattern repeats. Even a rough seasonal index helps explain a bump that is not a trend."),
]

# Side questions for handover-v3. No planted facts, no digits: the v3 chats quote
# real issue text, so a stray number or filename here would leak into the answer key.
V3_FILLERS = [
    ("Side question: should the commit message name the symptom or the fix?",
     "Name the fix, and mention the symptom in the body if a reviewer would not guess it. A subject line that only says the error is hard to scan in the log."),
    ("Is it worth splitting the formatting cleanup out of the bugfix commit?",
     "Yes. A formatting-only commit first keeps the diff of the real change readable. Reviewers otherwise spend their time on whitespace."),
    ("How do I mark a test as expected to fail until the fix lands?",
     "Prefer a skip with a comment that names the ticket, and delete the skip in the same change that fixes the bug. A permanent expected-failure marker hides regressions later."),
    ("Can I rebase onto the latest main before I open the pull request?",
     "Yes, if you have not asked anyone to review yet. Once review has started, merge main in instead so you do not force-push over comments."),
    ("What belongs in the pull request description besides the summary?",
     "How to reproduce the failure, what you changed, and how you checked it. A link to the discussion is enough for the long background."),
    ("Should I add a changelog entry for a bug that only shows up in an unusual setup?",
     "Yes, if a user could hit it. One sentence on the symptom and the version that fixes it is enough. Skip the changelog only for a pure refactor."),
    ("A reviewer asked me to rename a local variable. Does that need its own commit?",
     "No. Fold it into the commit they were reviewing if you have not merged yet. A separate commit for a rename makes the history noisier than the change."),
    ("How should I quote a traceback in a chat like this one?",
     "Keep the exception type, the message, and the two frames that are in our code. Drop the rest. A full dump buries the line that matters."),
    ("Is a draft pull request useful before the tests pass?",
     "Yes, when you want an early look at the approach. Say in the description that it is not ready, so nobody reviews it as finished work."),
    ("What's a good way to leave a note for myself in the branch?",
     "Put it in the pull request description, not in a comment inside the code, unless the next reader of that line still needs it. Code comments outlive the branch."),
    ("Do I need to update the contributor guide for a one-line fix?",
     "Only if the guide describes the behaviour you changed. A bugfix that matches the guide already does not need a docs edit."),
    ("Someone suggested I vendor a helper instead of importing it. When is that reasonable?",
     "When the helper is a few lines and pulling the dependency would be the larger change. If you might need the rest of that library later, import it and pin the version."),
    ("How do I keep a long debugging session out of the final diff?",
     "Stash or discard the print statements before you commit, and keep the one assertion that failed. The history should show the fix, not the search."),
    ("Is it fine to test only the function I changed and skip the suite locally?",
     "Run the test that failed first, then the module around it. Run the full suite before you ask for review if the change touches shared code."),
    ("What should I do if the same failure passes on my machine?",
     "Write down the version, the platform, and the exact command that failed for the reporter. Then try to match those before changing the code."),
    ("Can you remind me how to find which commit introduced a bad line?",
     "Bisect between a known good revision and the bad one, with a test command that exits non-zero on the bug. Keep the script short so each step is automatic."),
    ("A teammate wants this fix on the stable branch too. How do I do that cleanly?",
     "Land it on the main branch first, then cherry-pick the same commit onto the stable branch. Note the cherry-pick in the pull request so the two reviews stay linked."),
    ("Should the new test live next to the code or in a separate tests package?",
     "Follow the package you are editing. Putting one test in a new place makes it easy to miss when someone runs the usual command."),
    ("How much of the reproduction script should I paste into the ticket?",
     "The smallest script that still fails, with the import and the call. Leave out the data that is not needed to trigger the error."),
    ("Is it okay to depend on dict ordering in a test?",
     "Only if the language version you support guarantees it and the order is part of the behaviour. Otherwise compare as a set or sort both sides."),
    ("What's your view on asserting the full error message?",
     "Assert the exception type and a stable substring. Full-message asserts break when someone improves the wording and the bug is still fixed."),
    ("Do I need a benchmark for a change this small?",
     "Not unless the report is about speed. A correctness fix with a regression test is the right bar. Mention performance only if you measured a difference."),
    ("How should I handle a review comment I disagree with?",
     "Reply with the reason and the constraint you are protecting. If you still disagree after one exchange, ask for a short call rather than a long thread."),
    ("Can I close the ticket before the release ships?",
     "Close it when the fix is merged, and name the version that will contain it if you know. Leaving it open until the release makes the queue lie about what is left."),
    ("What's a sensible timeout for a test that starts a subprocess?",
     "Long enough for a slow machine in continuous integration, and short enough that a hang fails the job. Print the command on failure so the log shows what stalled."),
    ("Should I pin the tool versions in the test environment?",
     "Pin what the failure depends on, and leave the rest to the project's existing lock. A new pin in a bugfix is noise unless an old version caused the bug."),
    ("How do I describe a behaviour change that only affects an edge case?",
     "Say who hits it and what they see before and after. Readers can then tell whether their own code is in that case."),
    ("Is a property-based test worth it here?",
     "Only if you can state an invariant in one sentence, such as two calls returning the same result. Otherwise an example from the report is clearer."),
    ("A flaky test failed on the pull request and passed on rerun. What now?",
     "Look at whether your change touches what it covers. If it does not, rerun and mention the flake. If it does, treat the flake as your bug."),
    ("How do I keep secrets out of a fixture file?",
     "Use an obvious fake value and a comment that it is fake. A realistic-looking token gets copied into screenshots and tickets."),
    ("Should the fix include a deprecation warning for the old behaviour?",
     "Only if callers could have been relying on the bug. A crash is not behaviour anyone should have caught and handled."),
    ("What do I put in the test docstring?",
     "The situation and the expected result, in one or two lines. The name can carry the rest. A long docstring goes stale."),
    ("Can I use a skip if an optional dependency is missing?",
     "Yes, skip that test with a message naming the dependency. Do not fail the suite on a machine that never claimed to have it."),
    ("How do I make a regression test fail for the right reason?",
     "Assert the outcome the user cares about, not an internal counter. If the outcome is right and the counter changed, the test should pass."),
    ("Is it useful to add a comment pointing at the ticket inside the code?",
     "One comment at the non-obvious line is useful. A comment that only repeats the ticket title is not. Link the ticket from the test name or the commit."),
    ("What's the least I should check before I push?",
     "The new test fails on the old code and passes on the new code, and the module's existing tests still pass. That is the check that the test is real."),
    ("Someone asked for a feature flag around this fix. Do we need one?",
     "Not for a bug that returns the wrong result. A flag is for a change you may need to turn off for a caller who wants the old result."),
    ("How should I name the branch?",
     "Use the ticket id and a few words for the symptom. Avoid a branch name that only says 'fix' because you will have several of those."),
    ("Do I need to update type annotations if the runtime behaviour changes?",
     "Update them if they describe the value you changed. Leave unrelated annotation cleanups for another change."),
    ("What's a good first reply when a report is missing a version?",
     "Ask for the version and the smallest snippet that fails, and say what you have already tried. Guessing the version wastes a round trip."),
    ("Can two tests share a fixture that opens a file?",
     "Yes if they only read it. If one test writes, give it a copy so the other test does not depend on order."),
    ("How do I avoid a huge expected string in the test?",
     "Assert the part that was wrong, such as one clause of the output. A full-string snapshot breaks on harmless formatting."),
    ("Should I mention alternative approaches in the pull request?",
     "Yes, briefly, including the one you rejected and why. Reviewers otherwise suggest it again."),
    ("What if the bug only happens when two features are used together?",
     "The test should use both, in the same order the report did. Testing each feature alone will pass and miss the bug."),
    ("Is there a preference for helper functions inside a test module?",
     "A helper is good when three tests would otherwise paste the same setup. Keep it private to the module and name it for the setup, not for the assertion."),
]
